PCA was conducted on the cortical thickness data: 23 PCs explained 90% of the data.  CCA  was  performed  using  the  seven  neuropsychological variables as dataset U and the 23 cortical thickness PCs as dataset V ; Figure 1 shows heatmaps of each dataset and a scatterplot of the first canonical correlation, which had a strong linear correlation of 0.860. Pillai's Trace suggested that this was not statistically significant ( p = 0.112). Results for the remaining canonical correlations are provided in Data S1. A permutation test with n = 10,000 permutations also suggested the correlation was not significant but was trending towards significance ( p = 0.093).

Table  2  displays  the  canonical  loadings  and  cross-  loadings for each cognitive variable from dataset U .  DSST (minus Symbol Copy) and TMT-  B (minus TMT-  A) were most strongly associated with  the  first  canonical  variates U1 and V1 .  Figure  4  illustrates the  association  between  cortical  thickness  of  each  region  and the cognitive dataset U .  Three regions were most strongly associated  with  the  first  cognitive  canonical  variate U1 :  left  inferior temporal  ( rho = 0.406, p = 0.002),  right  entorhinal  ( rho = 0.380, p = 0.004)  and  right  temporal  pole  areas  ( rho = 0.334, p = 0.012). Three other regions did not reach the threshold of 0.3 but had significant p -  values:  right  inferior  temporal  ( rho = 0.286, p = 0.032), right pars orbitalis ( rho = 0.268, p = 0.046) and right superior temporal ( rho = 0.297, p = 0.026) areas. The full list of cross-  loadings is provided in Data S1.

